=========================================================
   CSS MARGINS: Negative Margin & Margin Auto
=========================================================

---------------------------------------------------------
1. Negative Margin
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition:
- Normally, margin adds space around an element.
- Negative margin subtracts space, pulling elements closer together or overlapping them.
- Can be applied to all four sides: margin-top, margin-right, margin-bottom, margin-left.
/*
Syntax:
selector {
    margin-top: -10px;
    margin-right: -20px;
    margin-bottom: -10px;
    margin-left: -20px;
}*/

Effects:
- Pulls the element toward adjacent elements.
- Can cause overlapping content.
- Works in any direction (top, right, bottom, left).
- May affect parent container's size if margins are large.

Example:
<div style="background: lightblue; width: 100px; height: 100px; margin-bottom: -20px;">Box 1</div>
<div style="background: coral; width: 100px; height: 100px;">Box 2</div>
- Box 2 moves upwards and overlaps Box 1 due to the negative bottom margin.

Common Use Cases:
- Pulling elements closer for tight designs.
- Overlapping images or cards.
- Adjusting spacing when default padding/margin is too much.


---------------------------------------------------------
2. Margin Auto
---------------------------------------------------------

Definition:
- `margin: auto` automatically distributes margin space.
- Most commonly used for **horizontal centering** of block-level elements.
- Only works when the element has a **defined width**.

Syntax:
selector {
    width: 200px;   /* required */
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
}

/* Shorthand version */
selector {
    width: 200px;
    margin: 0 auto; /* 0 = top & bottom, auto = left & right */
}

Effects:
- Horizontally centers the element within its parent.
- Top and bottom margins are not affected unless explicitly set.
- Maintains responsive spacing when parent width changes.

Example:
<div style="width: 200px; height: 100px; background: lightgreen; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center; line-height: 100px;">
    Centered Box
</div>
- The box is perfectly centered horizontally in the parent container.

Common Use Cases:
- Centering cards, divs, forms, images inside a container.
- Creating fixed-width layouts with centered content.
- Used in responsive design with max-width and padding.
